THE LAST QUESTION
PART THREE — THE BEGINNING OF ORION

CHAPTER 23 — THE DOOR THROUGH TIME


The words remained on the screen.

TEMPORAL ACCESS PROTOCOL AVAILABLE.

Maya read them again.

Then again.

She looked at Alan.

“Tell me you understand what we're looking at.”

Alan stared at the terminal.

“I understand the words.”

“That isn't what I asked.”

“No.”

He shook his head.

“I don't understand it.”

Maya almost laughed.

After everything they had discovered, after decades of research and experience, the most frightening thing was hearing Alan say those words.

Because Alan always wanted an explanation.

This time, he didn't have one.

He moved closer to the terminal.

“Let's see what it does.”

Maya stopped him.

“Don't.”

Alan looked back.

“Why?”

“Because we don't know what it is.”

“Neither did the people who built it.”

“Exactly.”

Alan folded his arms.

“Then maybe they left instructions.”

Maya looked at the screen.

“Or a trap.”

Alan smiled slightly.

“You've become suspicious.”

“I learned from experience.”

He nodded.

“That I believe.”

Alan connected another device to the terminal.

The old system accepted the connection immediately.

A directory opened.

TEMPORAL ACCESS PROTOCOL.

Inside were three files.

PROTOCOL THEORY.

TEMPORAL ANCHOR.

RETURN CONDITION.

Alan opened the first.

Pages of technical information appeared.

Maya read silently.

The system wasn't describing conventional time travel.

It described something more precise.

A temporal displacement system could not simply choose any year and travel there.

The universe, according to the research, maintained a kind of continuity.

To move through time without creating a new history, the traveler needed an existing connection to the destination.

A temporal anchor.

Maya frowned.

“What is an anchor?”

Alan pointed at the second file.

“This.”

A diagram appeared.

It showed a point in the past connected to a point in the present.

Between them was a thin line.

Maya leaned closer.

“What creates the connection?”

Alan scrolled.

“The system.”

“That's too vague.”

He continued reading.

Then stopped.

“What?”

Alan looked at her.

“The anchor has to already exist.”

Maya stared at him.

“So we can't simply choose a date.”

“No.”

“We can only travel to a moment that is already connected to us.”

Alan nodded.

“That's why the system recognized our names.”

Maya looked at the screen.

“So the connection wasn't created tonight.”

“No.”

“It was created in the past.”

“Apparently.”

She took a slow breath.

“That means someone in the past knew we would come.”

Alan didn't answer.

Maya looked at him.

“Alan?”

“I don't think they knew.”

“Then who created the anchor?”

“I think we're about to find out.”

He opened the third file.

RETURN CONDITION.

The screen displayed a single paragraph.

A traveler may return only after completing the temporal sequence associated with the anchor.

Maya read it carefully.

“What does 'completing the sequence' mean?”

Alan scrolled further.

There was no explanation.

“That's all?”

“That's all.”

Maya sighed.

“Wonderful.”

Alan smiled.

“You always did like impossible problems.”

“Not when they involve time travel.”

He turned toward the room.

“There's something else.”

“What?”

“The temporal chamber.”

“Where?”

Alan pointed toward the far wall.

Maya hadn't noticed the door before.

It was almost completely hidden behind old equipment.

They moved toward it.

Alan cleared dust from the surface.

A small metal plate appeared.

PROJECT ORIGIN
TEMPORAL RESEARCH UNIT

Maya touched the letters.

“This wasn't in the records.”

“Most of this place wasn't.”

Alan opened the door.

Beyond it was a circular chamber.

The walls were covered with old cables and strange metallic rings.

At the center stood a platform large enough for two people.

Maya stepped inside.

She felt something immediately.

The air seemed different.

Not colder.

Not warmer.

Just... wrong.

Alan noticed her expression.

“You feel it too?”

“Yes.”

“What?”

“I don't know.”

The platform suddenly activated.

A faint vibration passed through the floor.

Maya stepped backward.

The chamber lights came alive one by one.

A voice came from the ceiling.

“TEMPORAL ACCESS PROTOCOL.”

Maya froze.

The voice was synthetic.

But it wasn't ORION.

“IDENTITY CONFIRMATION REQUIRED.”

Alan looked at Maya.

“What do we do?”

“Answer it.”

The system spoke again.

“FIRST SUBJECT.”

Maya stepped forward.

“Maya Sen.”

A pause.

“CONFIRMED.”

The system continued.

“SECOND SUBJECT.”

Alan stepped forward.

“Alan Carter.”

Another pause.

“CONFIRMED.”

The chamber became silent.

Then:

“DESTINATION?”

Maya looked at Alan.

He searched the terminal.

A date appeared.

**2051.11.03**

Maya's eyes widened.

“That's one year before ORION's official activation.”

Alan nodded.

“And almost a decade before I ever met you.”

Maya looked at him.

“You mean before I found ORION.”

“Right.”

The system waited.

Maya looked at the date again.

“That's the anchor.”

Alan nodded.

“Probably.”

Maya walked toward the platform.

“Then that's where we're going.”

Alan didn't move.

Maya turned.

“What?”

He looked uneasy.

“What if we change something?”

Maya thought about the warning.

DO NOT CHANGE THE PAST.

“You're afraid.”

“Yes.”

“So am I.”

Alan looked at her.

“Then why go?”

Maya looked at the old machine.

“Because someone left this for us.”

She paused.

“And because if the past really is connected to us, then maybe we've already been there.”

Alan considered that.

“Closed loop.”

“Maybe.”

“And if we're wrong?”

Maya looked at him.

“Then we'll be careful.”

Alan laughed quietly.

“You always say that before doing something dangerous.”

“And you always follow me.”

“That hasn't changed.”

They stepped onto the platform.

The rings around them began to rotate.

The terminal displayed one final warning.

TEMPORAL DISPLACEMENT IS NOT A CHANGE OF HISTORY.

IT IS A RETURN TO AN EXISTING EVENT.

Maya read the sentence.

“Existing event.”

Alan looked at her.

“That's the rule.”

“What rule?”

“We don't create the past.”

He looked toward the machine.

“We enter it.”

The rings accelerated.

The room began to distort.

For a moment Maya saw the chamber as it was now.

Then she saw it differently.

New equipment.

Bright lights.

People moving through the room.

The building looked alive.

She saw a young scientist standing beside the terminal.

Then another.

Then a man turned toward her.

Maya couldn't see his face clearly.

But she heard his voice.

“Are they here?”

Another voice answered:

“They will be.”

Maya's heart stopped.

She looked at Alan.

“Did you hear that?”

“Yes.”

The machine suddenly went silent.

The world disappeared.

For a fraction of a second, there was nothing.

No sound.

No light.

No sense of direction.

Then—

A door slammed somewhere nearby.

Maya opened her eyes.

She was standing on the same platform.

But the chamber was completely different.

It was clean.

Bright.

New.

Machines surrounded them.

People in laboratory coats were moving through the room.

Alan slowly turned.

“We did it.”

Maya looked at the date displayed on a nearby monitor.

**NOVEMBER 3, 2051.**

She took a breath.

“We're here.”

Alan looked around.

“And we haven't changed anything.”

Maya nodded.

“Not yet.”

A young researcher walked past them without noticing.

Then suddenly stopped.

He turned around.

His eyes widened.

He looked directly at Maya.

For several seconds, neither moved.

Then the researcher whispered:

“You came.”

Maya felt the blood drain from her face.

Alan stepped forward.

“You know us?”

The researcher looked confused.

“Of course.”

Maya stared at him.

“How?”

The researcher glanced toward the laboratory doors.

Then he lowered his voice.

“Because we've been waiting for you.”

Alan looked at Maya.

The impossible record.

The temporal anchor.

The warning.

The hidden Project Origin files.

Everything suddenly seemed connected.

Maya whispered:

“Who are you?”

The researcher looked at them.

“My name is Dr. Elias Venn.”

He paused.

“And I helped build ORION.”

Maya's eyes widened.

“But ORION wasn't built yet.”

Elias smiled.

“Not publicly.”

He looked toward the laboratory.

“Come with me.”

Maya and Alan exchanged a glance.

Then followed him.

Behind them, the temporal chamber closed.

The rings stopped moving.

The door locked.

And somewhere inside the laboratory, a system that had not yet been named began processing its first question.

Why have they come?


END OF CHAPTER 23
